{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_011CgMnzCdEftCu5ut8dSCn1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PtKh6aT14sm1Rknxo8dzbT","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"[REDACTED:local-path:a2f23216d85d]/mnt/outputs/artifacts/deck-review-acmecorp/handoff/cw-dr-smoke-0a1b2c3d/checklist_output.json","content":"{
  "slide_reviews_read_token": "b4eadab39dcb",
  "items": [
    {
      "id": "purpose_clear",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "The title slide states 'Acmecorp — AI-native developer tools for indie SaaS founders', which names the category (developer tools) and the ICP (indie SaaS founders) but omits any quantified outcome. Sequoia's framework requires a single sentence in the form '[Category] for [ICP] that delivers [quantified outcome]'; the outcome half is missing.",
      "notes": "Rewrite the title tagline as a full value-proposition sentence: 'Acmecorp gives indie SaaS founders one CLI that cuts developer-tooling integration from 20+ hours per week to under 2, so they ship instead of stitching tools together.' Name what changes, not just who it is for."
    },
    {
      "id": "headlines_carry_story",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Slides 2 and 4 carry conclusion headlines ('Problem: 20+ hours/week wasted on developer tooling integration' and 'Traction: $8K MRR, 24 paying customers, 18% MoM growth'). Slides 1, 3, 5, and 6 use topic labels ('Acmecorp — AI-native developer tools for indie SaaS founders', 'Solution: One CLI wrapping GitHub, Linear, Stripe, and Vercel', 'Team: 2 co-founders, ex-Stripe and ex-Notion', 'Ask: $1.0M–$2.0M raise with three runway scenarios'). DocSend and Sequoia both advise that each headline should be a conclusion an investor can act on.",
      "notes": "Rewrite the topic headlines on slides 1, 3, 5, and 6 as conclusions. For example: slide 3 becomes 'Acmecorp cuts tooling overhead from 20+ hours per week to under 2'; slide 5 becomes 'Two founders who built the exact stack Acmecorp replaces'; slide 6 becomes 'We are raising $2.0M to reach $50K MRR by Q4 2027.'"
    },
    {
      "id": "narrative_arc_present",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "The six slides follow Problem → Solution → Traction → Team → Ask, which is the correct skeleton. However, the arc has significant gaps: there is no Why Now, no Market, no Competition, no Business Model, and no GTM section. Sequoia, DocSend, and YC all treat a complete Problem → Solution → Why Now → Proof → Market → Ask arc as the minimum for a seed deck.",
      "notes": "Add the missing middle sections (market sizing, why now, competition, business model, GTM) so the deck flows from problem through a complete investment thesis to the ask. The existing six slides form the right skeleton; the substance between traction and ask is absent."
    },
    {
      "id": "strongest_proof_early",
      "status": "pass",
      "evidence": "The strongest proof point — '$8K MRR, 24 paying customers, 18% MoM growth (last 4 months)' — appears on slide 4. DocSend data shows that if a reader reaches slide 4, 82% finish the deck; placing the best traction signal here is the right structural choice."
    },
    {
      "id": "story_stands_alone",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "Each of the six slides contains 9–23 words of substantive content. An investor reading the deck without narration would encounter: no explanation of the market size, no competitive landscape, no business model, no acquisition channel, no product visual, and no financial projections. Six sections the deck is silent on means a reader cannot follow the investment thesis. DocSend research shows investors spend roughly 2:30 on a deck; at this content density, that time yields almost no information.",
      "notes": "Expand each existing slide to a full standalone slide with supporting data, and add the six missing sections (why now, market, competition, business model, GTM, financials). An investor who has never spoken with you should be able to read the deck and understand the full opportunity and the ask without any verbal context."
    },
    {
      "id": "problem_quantified",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "The problem slide identifies the user (indie SaaS founders) and quantifies the pain (20+ hours/week wasted on developer tooling integration), meeting the core Sequoia requirement. However, no source is cited for the figure, and no customer quotes or concrete examples are present. a16z's pitch-deck guidance notes that a problem slide without customer discovery evidence reads as a hypothesis, not a finding.",
      "notes": "Add the source for '20+ hours/week' — a customer survey, interview, or a direct statement from one of your 24 paying customers. Include one customer quote in their own words describing what they were doing before Acmecorp. 'Eight of our 24 paying customers reported spending 20+ hours per week on integration before switching' is more credible and more specific than the current unsourced figure."
    },
    {
      "id": "solution_shows_workflow",
